WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.980
Look out here, there's a little robot. There it is.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:05.020
I went to Japan to visit Woven City,

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.720
the city built by Toyota

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.010
to test new mobility technologies,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.140
from self-driving cars

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.620
to AI vision systems.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.680
And it's not a fake city,

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.560
real people will live there, scientists,

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.780
startup founders, researchers, and it will eventually cover

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.560
the equivalent of 100 football fields.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.670
However, I arrived in Tokyo

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.000
a couple of days early

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.000
to play the tourist for a bit.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.820
I bribed some dogs in a dog cafe,

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.040
I ate pancakes and a ton of sushi.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.919
And then my real immersion

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.600
into the Toyota world began.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.540
It's been three incredibly intense days.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.040
On the first day, I went to Nagoya

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.300
to discover the history of the company and its cars.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.490
On the second, I finally arrived at Woven City,

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.260
where I also met Daisuke Toyoda,

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.880
the son of Akio Toyoda, the current president of the company.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.240
And on the third day back

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.060
to Woven City for the Inventor Garage,

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:46.769
the laboratory where they test

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.020
the prototypes of these new technologies. Let's begin.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.540
Good morning guys, first real day here with Toyota.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.260
We agreed to meet up at 9:05

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.680
right back here where the groups are

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.040
because we'll be leaving our luggage,

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.880
they will take it directly to the next hotel for us,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.400
while we'll be free to travel around Japan.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.700
The first stop today is Nagoya,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.940
so we are changing cities, we'll head straight to Nagoya

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.239
on the Shinkansen to see

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.500
the Toyota museum.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.880
We got on the Shinkansen, heading to Nagoya.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.280
This is the historic Shinkansen line,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.340
it left exactly at 10:09 as stated on the ticket.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.020
In Japan, the average delay for these trains

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.980
is 36 seconds per year. Pretty efficient.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.220
Officially arrived in Nagoya.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.140
Do you know what Nagoya is famous for?

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:29.740
I don't know.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:30.420
Because it's the city

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:31.520
where Toyota was born.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.320
That I know.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.080
In fact, just a stone's throw away from here

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.860
there's another city called Toyota City, literally.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.320
We arrived at the Toyota museum by bus.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.830
Here there's also an Italian restaurant

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.600
where we had a light lunch,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.780
it wasn't exactly Italian, there was rice with meat,

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:47.780
but somewhere in between.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.360
And now we're taking a one-hour tour here at the museum

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.160
to find out a bit about how Toyota was founded,

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:53.520
its history up to the present day

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.590
and to see the context needed to understand tomorrow,

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.020
where the future of the company will be.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.020
Right in front of me is

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.280
one of the very first Toyota machines,

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:01.400
which are not cars

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.580
but rather spinning machines,

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.180
because that's exactly how Toyota started.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.620
Now I'll check out the history a bit.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.240
The founder of Toyota is Sakichi Toyoda,

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.200
who founded his automatic loom company

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.800
which was called Toyoda with a D.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:24.740
Only later, when his son changed the company

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.440
transforming it into an engine company,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.620
did the company name also change

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.510
to Toyota with a T,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.400
partly because in Japan

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.320
it is written with eight strokes, a lucky number.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.540
This machine back here

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.570
is the automatic loom invented by Sakichi himself.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:36.650
It's a non-stop loom,

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:37.880
because the previous looms

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.880
would stop when the thread ran out

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.980
and this creates industrial automation for the first time,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:44.440
meaning the shuttle containing

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.180
the thread is changed automatically.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.040
And here a concept is introduced

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.680
that will become the DNA of Toyota

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.520
and that's why this machine is so important,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.340
namely the concept of intelligent automation. In Japanese, "jidoka".

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.920
This is because Sakichi was obsessed with efficiency.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:00.540
In fact, the whole lean manufacturing

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.280
that is now used in most

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.420
factories around the world was born thanks to Toyota.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.020
The concept of just in time,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.200
of kaizen and continuous improvement

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.440
are all concepts born with Toyota. Let's briefly explain them.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.080
Lean manufacturing, or streamlined production,

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.360
is a production system

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.440
developed in the '80s and '90s by Toyota

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.480
to maximize the value generated

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.260
and it's based on two core principles:

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.200
just in time,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.080
which involves producing only what has been sold

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.820
or what is highly expected to be sold,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.620
so as to avoid waste and warehouse management

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.700
and kaizen, which is the principle of continuous improvement

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.710
that consists of encouraging all workers

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.430
to find small improvements in the product

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.560
and in the production system

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.220
which, added up, make a difference.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.000
These principles introduced by Toyota

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.840
have revolutionized the industry worldwide. Let's move on.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.600
Then the museum continues here

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.960
telling a bit of the history of weaving pre-Toyota,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.960
like the cotton arriving in Japan,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.980
how cotton is spun in a traditional way,

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.820
and how machines industrialized this process.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.380
And right here is a series of machines

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.980
that tell the story of this exact process.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.060
Jaky, what's going on?

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.800
He's showing us how the automation

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.620
of the first automatic machinery works and it's so cool.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.730
All these series of inventions

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.760
that were made to automate spinning

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.500
at a certain point were entirely sold to the British,

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.479
to the biggest British automatic machinery company.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.140
All the patents were sold

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.100
and with the money obtained from the sale of these patents

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:29.440
Toyota financed its transition into the engine market.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:30.640
It's a bit like if today Toyota

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.620
sold its entire automotive division

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.040
to finance the robotics division. A truly crazy gamble.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.240
Toyota, however, didn't stop making looms,

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:39.420
they sold the patents

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.380
but they also continued that business,

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:42.530
dividing the company in two,

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.360
the engine division and the loom division,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.660
which then evolved into

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.860
what is today Toyota Industries.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.720
Here we have a fantastic map of the entire Toyota Group.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.980
The old Toyota Loom Works, which is the loom division,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.220
becomes Toyota Industries

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.300
and the Toyota Motor Company remains Toyota Motor Corporation.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.720
And as you can see, the research and development division becomes Woven,

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.300
which is Toyota's frontier technology division,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.350
and tomorrow in fact we are heading right to Woven City,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.700
acting as if it were a city

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.300
where Toyota does research and development

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.320
and tests frontier technology.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.780
From this side we move

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.960
from looms to cars. Let's take a look.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.880
The reason why they transition from looms to cars

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.140
is because the founder's son, Kiichiro,

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.540
after a trip to the United States and Europe,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.860
realizes how popular cars are becoming

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:37.420
and decides that this is the market to enter

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.513
and so he starts studying the engine.

00:05:41.513 --> 00:05:42.980
From here on out

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.520
you can see how they created the first engines,

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.240
overcoming the initial technological challenges,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.830
setting up the first production lines,

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.320
going through all the technological advancements of engines, of models,

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.540
all the way to electric ones, hydrogen ones, and so on.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.220
By the way, on the walls it was quite funny

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.240
because there was probably the whole explanation

00:05:58.260 --> 00:05:59.680
of the history in the form of a manga.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.300
Unfortunately it was in Japanese so I didn't understand it.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:02.260
And then the largest section

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.360
was dedicated to the simplified reconstruction

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.200
of the actual factory as it is right now.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:07.860
So there was the actual creation phase

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.760
of the parts, like the cylinders,

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.760
the assembly of the various parts with mechanical arms,

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:16.520
the painting of the bodywork, the mating of the bodywork with the engine.

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.100
All things that drive me crazy in a good way.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.310
Unfortunately, during this visit

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:22.189
we won't be able to see the actual Toyota factory,

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.660
one of the many that exist here,

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:24.900
and so I'm glad to have had

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:26.500
a sneak peek in this museum.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.120
Then there were also all the possible

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.620
and imaginable car models

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:30.700
and there wasn't enough time

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:33.240
to listen to the audioguide on every single model.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.160
At that point we went to a new museum,

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.600
this one also about cars, but this time not just Toyota,

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.140
but cars in general.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.870
In fact, it opened with a fantastic Toyota Model AA,

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.300
of which only one preserved example exists in the world today,

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:49.860
not the one you see here, which is a replica.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:53.730
After that, the museum starts with the very first generation of cars,

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.130
which were basically carriages with an engine,

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:57.320
that carriage by carriage

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:58.360
evolved more and more.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.620
There were already carriages back then with electric motors,

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.700
with steam engines, with petrol engines.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:04.860
They showed us various models,

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:08.310
like the very first mass-produced car,

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.900
which is the Ford Model T,

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.180
moving then year by year

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:12.440
to increasingly modern cars.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:14.920
It was so cool to see all the small modifications

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:17.440
that led to the aesthetics that cars have today.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.580
There were the gigantic American cars,

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.460
the smaller European cars,

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:23.400
there were the very first race cars,

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.000
all the way up to recent models

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:26.380
with a hydrogen engine.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:27.780
Favorite car from this visit?

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:29.840
Probably the historical ones downstairs

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.620
which are one crazier than the other.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.000
After a very nice little dinner

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:39.020
grilled with everyone

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.880
I took a walk and then went to sleep.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.300
Now I'm back on the Shinkansen heading to Mishima,

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:46.140
which, however, isn't today's final stop

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.360
because we are going straight to Woven City.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.099
Unfortunately the weather isn't helping us,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:51.160
but nothing can stop us.

00:07:52.599 --> 00:07:54.000
After that, as usual, we took

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:56.020
the bus that brought us to lunch.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:57.880
It's 11:30, a very early lunch,

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.920
very Japanese. Finally a truly typical spot.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.800
By the way, Mishima is not a tourist spot in Japan,

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:04.800
it's an industrial city.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:06.680
For lunch, a few little appetizers

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.120
and then this sort of soup

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:09.740
that we will mix with the meat, with the rice,

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:11.500
with the miso soup, a bit of everything.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:12.900
The veggies have arrived too.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.200
Very good lunch and after a few minutes

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.440
on the bus we finally arrived at Woven City.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:22.200
Here there are journalists and creators from all over Europe

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:24.060
and also people from the Toyota team.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:25.840
Right in front of me is a series of logos

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.140
of all the companies participating in the project.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:31.080
It actually looks exactly like a city in every way.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:32.660
There are also means of transport,

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.180
there are traffic lights, lanes for electric scooters.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.919
This structure too is built in a way

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:38.720
that cars can pass right through it,

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.440
precisely because if you want to test all the technologies

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:43.540
in the mobility world, you need a place

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:45.320
that is tailor-made specifically for this,

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.620
basically like a real city.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:47.840
Here we have a piece of the map,

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:49.420
we are right here at the Welcome Center,

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.780
right here is the Kakesan Invention Hub.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.520
Kakesan is a Japanese word meaning multiplication,

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:56.240
kind of like kaizen and many others

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:57.480
which are the keywords

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:58.960
that made Toyota famous.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.720
Here is the Weavers Center, and we are the weavers,

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.360
all the people who come and test these technologies.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:05.720
Here is the convenience store

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:07.020
and then it definitely continues over here

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.380
because there are a ton of other areas,

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.140
like for instance a garage for innovators

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.280
that is already partnered with various companies.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.540
There's a difference between an inventor and a weaver.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.360
The inventors are the ones who come here

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:17.820
to bring the technologies, and the weavers

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.540
are the ones living inside it, living their lives.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:21.040
So I am a weaver

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.080
and Jaki is clearly an inventor. Darn it!

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:26.540
However, Mr. Toyoda himself

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.620
said he is the master weaver.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:29.940
In short, the concept is simple:

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:32.380
they transformed what would be a research hub

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:33.500
into an actual, real city

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.320
to be able to test absolutely everything.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.140
I imagine they'll tell us exactly what now,

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.940
but I can already see streetlights, electric scooters, buses, traffic lights.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:43.980
I imagine everything here is full of sensors, has multiple functions.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:44.900
I can't wait for them to tell us

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.760
exactly how it works around here.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.260
And as you know, I am truly

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.340
a city enthusiast, and have been for quite a while,

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:51.460
since I started traveling, really.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.980
I love trains, I love traveling by train,

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.860
I like mobility and so this project gets me pretty hyped.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.980
Look over here, the professional. Awesome, professional.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.360
Over here we have the convenience store,

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:05.860
there are snacks that aren't just for display,

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.180
but because during this period this city

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.440
will start to be populated by Toyota employees,

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:15.540
by startups, by scientists, researchers,

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.080
specifically to test its capabilities at 100%

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.580
and to leverage all the minds that come in here,

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:22.740
those famous weavers I mentioned earlier.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:24.940
Because of this, there are already quite a few things here.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:27.620
Over there, in fact, it really looks like a restaurant.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.960
Oh yeah, so all the people linked to the project stay here.

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:33.120
Phase two of the project is still under construction,

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:34.170
so it's not finished yet,

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:37.350
but it's starting to be fully operational.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:41.620
Here is a brief explanation of all the technologies.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:44.840
For instance, the e-palette, which is this minibus

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:45.880
that you also saw earlier,

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.790
which can act as transportation,

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:51.280
as a food and beverage service, and as entertainment.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.330
Then we have personal mobility vehicles,

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:56.080
which I think are these evolutions of electric scooters here,

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.420
and then of course car sharing

00:10:58.480 --> 00:10:59.460
with self-driving cars,

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:01.240
so the future of autonomous driving.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.800
Obviously, little robots carrying deliveries around.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.560
These already exist in some cities,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:06.660
but we still need to figure out

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:07.980
the best way to utilize them.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.940
Remote communication, little robots moving on their own,

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.020
carrying things. And here is a very long series

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:14.720
of initiatives and inventions,

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:18.330
like trying to create spaces with low pollen for example,

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.340
or the future of vending machines.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:28.760
We just listened to a speech by Mr. Toyoda Junior,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.320
who is the vice president of the entire company,

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:35.220
and the great-grandson of that Toyoda who transformed

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.840
the textile company into a car company.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:40.260
He's telling us the history of the company,

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:46.957
as well as the history of his great-great-grandfather.

00:11:46.957 --> 00:11:53.024
We said goodbye to Mr. Toyoda and now we're outside

00:11:53.064 --> 00:11:55.124
because they're finally showing us a few things.

00:11:55.224 --> 00:11:56.544
For example, they just told us

00:11:56.604 --> 00:11:59.824
that 100 people are already living in these houses here.

00:11:59.864 --> 00:12:02.324
Now they're taking us into this glass building.

00:12:03.704 --> 00:12:06.344
This is the Kagesan Invention Hub, where the Weavers,

00:12:06.444 --> 00:12:08.734
meaning the citizens, and the inventors,

00:12:08.884 --> 00:12:11.444
can collaborate to look for all the new technological

00:12:11.464 --> 00:12:12.704
solutions for mobility.

00:12:12.804 --> 00:12:14.624
Mobility doesn't simply mean

00:12:14.684 --> 00:12:16.184
moving from point A to point B,

00:12:16.404 --> 00:12:18.654
but anything related to movement,

00:12:18.724 --> 00:12:20.524
as Mr. Toyoda explained to us earlier.

00:12:20.644 --> 00:12:23.644
This is the wish list of the residents

00:12:23.724 --> 00:12:26.084
so that the inventors can work on these things.

00:12:26.224 --> 00:12:28.084
Here, for example, you can show me some inventors.

00:12:28.104 --> 00:12:30.204
Daido is a vending machine company.

00:12:30.464 --> 00:12:31.644
This is some of the feedback

00:12:31.824 --> 00:12:34.244
and here they are inventing the new models of vending machines.

00:12:34.544 --> 00:12:36.224
Jakidale has this one in his studio.

00:12:37.304 --> 00:12:40.144
These robots you see, that make the deliveries,

00:12:40.154 --> 00:12:41.824
they always travel underground here,

00:12:43.064 --> 00:12:44.824
beneath Woven City. Look out here,

00:12:44.864 --> 00:12:47.304
there's a little robot. There it is.

00:12:48.564 --> 00:12:50.634
A little robot. What did you find? What's in here?

00:12:50.924 --> 00:12:51.444
Karaoke.

00:12:51.864 --> 00:12:53.104
Ah, there's karaoke.

00:12:53.324 --> 00:12:55.184
Because they are designed to be modular

00:12:55.204 --> 00:12:56.724
and therefore adapt to all the various-

00:12:56.764 --> 00:12:58.554
Of course, they are adaptable. Here is karaoke,

00:12:58.624 --> 00:13:00.904
while over there is the kitchen for eating.

00:13:02.844 --> 00:13:05.864
Okay, we're outside. Here we have the supermarket. Check it out.

00:13:06.504 --> 00:13:08.544
I finally discovered what that little robot does.

00:13:08.684 --> 00:13:11.624
It's basically a valet parker that connects,

00:13:11.904 --> 00:13:14.924
I imagine through sensors, to your car and takes it away.

00:13:15.084 --> 00:13:17.344
In this demonstration there was a driver inside

00:13:17.424 --> 00:13:18.464
but who wasn't touching the steering wheel,

00:13:18.524 --> 00:13:20.244
I think it was solely for safety reasons,

00:13:20.284 --> 00:13:22.444
because it's actually made to function automatically.

00:13:22.504 --> 00:13:24.944
Imagine you took a ride sharing trip,

00:13:25.144 --> 00:13:27.264
you leave your car in the hands of the little robot,

00:13:27.384 --> 00:13:28.984
and then the robot takes care of putting it away.

00:13:29.024 --> 00:13:30.724
It's interesting because obviously if your car

00:13:30.764 --> 00:13:33.524
is already a self-driving car then you don't need it,

00:13:33.604 --> 00:13:35.084
but if your car is a standard car

00:13:35.164 --> 00:13:37.684
you just insert a box inside the car

00:13:37.764 --> 00:13:39.804
and it's as if that little robot transforms it

00:13:39.904 --> 00:13:41.604
into a self-driving car. Very cool.

00:13:41.664 --> 00:13:44.224
Then they showed us their pretty large electric scooters,

00:13:44.284 --> 00:13:45.024
with three wheels,

00:13:45.124 --> 00:13:47.564
and as a scooter enthusiast I absolutely wanted one.

00:13:47.684 --> 00:13:49.724
Then we stopped by the bar to check out

00:13:49.744 --> 00:13:51.564
their AI vision system

00:13:51.604 --> 00:13:52.784
and we had the opportunity

00:13:52.824 --> 00:13:54.324
to ask the CTO a couple of questions.

00:13:54.444 --> 00:13:56.624
After that we were exhausted and went to the hotel.

00:13:56.784 --> 00:13:58.304
We arrived at the hotel in a somewhat

00:13:58.343 --> 00:14:00.004
remote place here in Japan,

00:14:00.084 --> 00:14:01.604
but which is not just a hotel,

00:14:01.744 --> 00:14:04.664
it's also the home of the motorsport museum.

00:14:04.824 --> 00:14:07.904
It's called Fuji Speedway Hotel, not by chance,

00:14:08.084 --> 00:14:10.244
because it should look right out onto the track.

00:14:10.244 --> 00:14:11.344
I won't start filming

00:14:11.404 --> 00:14:12.804
all the vintage cars that are here right now

00:14:12.844 --> 00:14:14.464
because we'll be doing the tour shortly.

00:14:17.504 --> 00:14:20.044
At this moment I was at the Fuji Motorsports Museum,

00:14:20.204 --> 00:14:23.584
a museum dedicated to the history of Japanese and global motoring,

00:14:23.664 --> 00:14:25.284
specifically focusing on the racing side,

00:14:25.424 --> 00:14:28.284
with a ton of original models and racing legends.

00:14:28.384 --> 00:14:31.324
The Fuji Speedway is one of Japan's historic circuits,

00:14:31.444 --> 00:14:33.664
a Formula 1 track at the foot of Mount Fuji,

00:14:33.724 --> 00:14:35.214
famous for its incredibly long straight

00:14:35.304 --> 00:14:37.584
and for having hosted iconic GPs from the '70s

00:14:37.644 --> 00:14:39.244
up to the recent return of Formula 1.

00:14:39.404 --> 00:14:41.184
The hotel where I was staying is a luxury hotel,

00:14:41.284 --> 00:14:43.544
built directly inside the circuit complex,

00:14:43.684 --> 00:14:45.544
with rooms overlooking the track and Mount Fuji,

00:14:45.604 --> 00:14:47.404
which unfortunately I didn't see because it was a bit cloudy.

00:14:47.444 --> 00:14:50.264
The motorsport atmosphere is everywhere and there would even be direct access,

00:14:50.294 --> 00:14:53.424
provided there is racing activity.

00:14:57.564 --> 00:15:00.424
This is the original car that won in 2019.

00:15:00.584 --> 00:15:01.104
What did it win?

00:15:01.504 --> 00:15:02.164
It won Le Mans.

00:15:02.284 --> 00:15:04.064
With the legendary Fernando Alonso.

00:15:06.564 --> 00:15:08.764
The dinner was absolutely incredible.

00:15:08.784 --> 00:15:10.344
After that, this morning I left the room

00:15:10.354 --> 00:15:12.464
and we headed here, back to Woven City,

00:15:12.544 --> 00:15:14.624
to the Inventor Garage. We are in a different area

00:15:14.664 --> 00:15:16.104
compared to where the houses are.

00:15:16.144 --> 00:15:18.564
It's a gigantic hangar and now we'll find out exactly what it is.

00:15:18.624 --> 00:15:20.684
Now we're seated, they're going to give us a lesson, I think.

00:15:25.784 --> 00:15:27.884
The Inventor Garage is kind of like

00:15:27.944 --> 00:15:29.544
a laboratory within the laboratory.

00:15:29.644 --> 00:15:33.544
If the entire Woven City is a technology testing hub,

00:15:33.664 --> 00:15:35.693
like autonomous driving, artificial intelligence,

00:15:35.774 --> 00:15:38.604
the little robots, etc., the prototypes and testing

00:15:38.644 --> 00:15:40.904
of all these technologies are done right here.

00:15:40.964 --> 00:15:43.524
It's a gigantic place, here you only see one warehouse,

00:15:43.584 --> 00:15:44.744
let's say, of the various ones that are here.

00:15:44.784 --> 00:15:46.284
This used to be a former Toyota factory,

00:15:46.364 --> 00:15:49.304
which was later decommissioned right at the foot of Mount Fuji.

00:15:49.384 --> 00:15:52.364
The technologies I'm talking about are precisely autonomous driving,

00:15:52.464 --> 00:15:54.644
an artificial general intelligence software

00:15:54.684 --> 00:15:57.394
connected to all the sensors across the entire city,

00:15:57.564 --> 00:16:01.404
a smart vision system, the robots, the smart parking lots.

00:16:01.464 --> 00:16:03.504
Right now there's an event I'm participating in

00:16:03.544 --> 00:16:04.964
called Kagesan 2026

00:16:05.004 --> 00:16:06.484
and so this thing you see around me

00:16:06.543 --> 00:16:10.024
are all booths that explain each of these technologies to you.

00:16:10.084 --> 00:16:12.584
Jaki, what's your favorite technology today?

00:16:12.724 --> 00:16:16.044
Well, in my opinion, what's really interesting

00:16:16.284 --> 00:16:20.304
is that thing about using car parking spaces

00:16:20.384 --> 00:16:22.544
as energy storage systems.

00:16:22.624 --> 00:16:24.384
They already do it, they are already doing it.

00:16:24.524 --> 00:16:25.004
Crazy.

00:16:25.264 --> 00:16:27.004
What is your favorite technology here?

00:16:27.144 --> 00:16:30.804
Honestly speaking, data-driven automated driving is incredible.

00:16:30.864 --> 00:16:32.704
And also the AI vision engine

00:16:32.724 --> 00:16:34.344
that you'll see over here is incredible.

00:16:34.484 --> 00:16:36.104
I just came back from San Francisco,

00:16:36.384 --> 00:16:39.504
where you can actually live and use autonomous cars.

00:16:39.984 --> 00:16:40.904
What do you think about that?

00:16:41.004 --> 00:16:42.884
I live between here and the Bay Area,

00:16:43.124 --> 00:16:44.964
and I've ridden in many automated cars.

00:16:45.004 --> 00:16:46.384
I've been working on automated driving

00:16:46.484 --> 00:16:47.864
for the better part of 20 years now,

00:16:48.224 --> 00:16:49.824
so I've pretty much ridden in every single one.

00:16:49.964 --> 00:16:51.244
We are actually partnered with Waymo

00:16:51.324 --> 00:16:54.064
to achieve that vision of a zero-accident society.

00:16:54.144 --> 00:16:55.844
They told us a couple of things about this place

00:16:55.924 --> 00:16:58.324
before it was the Inventor Garage, when it was a factory.

00:16:58.384 --> 00:17:00.884
It had 7,000 employees, produced millions of cars

00:17:00.944 --> 00:17:01.824
and then it was decommissioned

00:17:01.924 --> 00:17:03.424
and now they're giving us a tour.

00:17:03.604 --> 00:17:06.784
In this area there was a gigantic stamping machine.

00:17:06.844 --> 00:17:08.384
Here there was a gigantic press

00:17:08.484 --> 00:17:10.094
and it's been transformed into a theater.

00:17:10.164 --> 00:17:12.554
Those stains you see, they tried to remove them multiple times,

00:17:12.604 --> 00:17:14.144
but they can't because they are the result

00:17:14.164 --> 00:17:16.364
of years and years of lubricating oil

00:17:16.424 --> 00:17:18.664
that seeped into the concrete.

00:17:18.964 --> 00:17:21.064
That means safety first in Japanese.

00:17:21.124 --> 00:17:21.994
Anzen Daichi.

00:17:26.664 --> 00:17:28.044
After that I took a look around,

00:17:28.064 --> 00:17:30.624
I finally tried the loom, the ancient one,

00:17:30.664 --> 00:17:34.244
the first machine built in 1890.

00:17:34.304 --> 00:17:36.364
Okay, I change foot and then I do it.

00:17:36.744 --> 00:17:40.464
One, two. Change foot. One, two, three.

00:17:41.024 --> 00:17:43.124
Then I tested the robot that makes coffee.

00:17:43.244 --> 00:17:45.364
I took a look at all the other technologies

00:17:45.404 --> 00:17:47.204
that were on display in the exhibition spaces.

00:17:47.224 --> 00:17:49.664
This was a machine that transported cars

00:17:49.684 --> 00:17:53.544
to take them to be painted, all the way to Joby's quadcopter,

00:17:53.564 --> 00:17:55.544
which is much bigger than it looks in the videos.

00:17:55.604 --> 00:17:57.784
Now a little break since we're having lunch soon.

00:17:57.824 --> 00:17:59.644
Toyota, after over a century of history,

00:17:59.724 --> 00:18:01.034
continues to look towards the future.

00:18:01.034 --> 00:18:03.004
Woven City is a sort of startup incubator

00:18:03.064 --> 00:18:05.544
in the form of a city, where innovators can test

00:18:05.584 --> 00:18:07.684
their technologies in a controlled environment

00:18:07.744 --> 00:18:09.424
but with real people using them.

00:18:09.464 --> 00:18:10.904
I had never seen anything like this,

00:18:10.944 --> 00:18:12.784
I'm curious to see what will come of it.

00:18:12.844 --> 00:18:14.824
Let me know what you think in the comments

00:18:14.884 --> 00:18:17.284
and now I'm going to get the book "The Toyota Way",

00:18:17.344 --> 00:18:18.234
which I haven't read yet

00:18:18.264 --> 00:18:19.944
but after this experience it's a must.

